# tools/cr/crypto_ed25519_scalar_witness.rish -- scalar arithmetic modulo the group order L authored in pure Rye: Season G's tenth rung, the
# hash-to-scalar step between GREEN SHA-512 and the GREEN scalar ladder. reduce folds a 64-byte little-endian value modulo L into a canonical
# 32-byte scalar; is_canonical answers whether a 32-byte encoding is already below L. Proven against algebraic known-answers and byte-for-byte
# against an independent implementation (Season G - Cryptography).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography - Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G names Ed25519 as the Lotus unblock;
# the scalar reduction mod L is the piece between SHA-512 and the group equation).
# Kin: crypto/ed25519_scalarmul.rye (the ladder that consumes a reduced scalar, GREEN) - crypto/sha512.rye (the hash whose 64-byte output this
# reduces, GREEN) - crypto/ed25519_decode.rye (the point decode verify also composes, GREEN). Monocypher (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is
# the parity TARGET, never a copied line -- our own composition, studied clean-room from RFC 8032 (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
#
# crypto/ed25519_scalar.rye reduces a 64-byte little-endian value modulo L = 2^252 + 27742317777372353535851937790883648493 (RFC 8032) by
# bytewise Horner from the most significant byte down, keeping the running residue below L by a run of subtractions proven to number fewer than
# 256 per byte; is_canonical answers whether a 32-byte encoding is the canonical representative below L. L's limbs are derived from its defining
# form in our own arithmetic, never pasted as a limb pattern. The primitive calls no std.crypto. Parity is proven at selftest against algebraic
# known-answers (0 reduces to 0, L reduces to 0, 0 is canonical and L is not) and Zig's own std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.scalar.reduce64 and
# rejectNonCanonical as an independent implementation, byte-for-byte and verdict-for-verdict across a spread of inputs. The vendored Monocypher
# source is a held network fetch, so Monocypher-source parity is an honest horizon rung. Purely local -- in verification the value reduced is the
# public SHA-512(R ‖ A ‖ M) and the encoding checked is the public signature S: no real key, no signing (Ed25519's signing stays its own gated
# round), no network, no funds, no real device; constant-time timing-safety a separate named horizon.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crypto_ed25519_scalar_witness.rish

say "crypto-ed25519-scalar: Language EN — Season G's tenth rung: scalar arithmetic modulo the group order L authored in pure Rye, the hash-to-scalar step between GREEN SHA-512 and the GREEN scalar ladder. Ed25519 verification hashes the commitment, the public key, and the message into a 64-byte value and reduces it modulo L to obtain the challenge scalar, and refuses a signature whose S is not already below L; this rung authors exactly reduce and is_canonical. It is pure arithmetic — no key, no signature, no identity, no custody gate — so with it every arithmetic piece the Ed25519 verify equation needs now stands, and verify becomes a composition over decode, the scalar ladder, the group law, and SHA-512."
say "crypto-ed25519-scalar: Style Radiant — the law is stated positively: reduce folds a 64-byte little-endian value modulo L = 2^252 + 27742317777372353535851937790883648493 (RFC 8032) by bytewise Horner from the most significant byte down, keeping the running residue below L by a run of subtractions proven to number fewer than 256 per byte; is_canonical answers whether a 32-byte encoding is the canonical representative below L. L's limbs are derived from its defining form in our own arithmetic — the small addend split from a u128, 2^252 placed by a shift — never pasted as a limb pattern."
say "crypto-ed25519-scalar: Lens TAME — the primitive calls no std.crypto; it asserts its contracts on every function and bounds its inner reduction loop by a named, proven constant. Parity holds against algebraic known-answers (0 reduces to 0, L reduces to 0, 0 is canonical and L is not) AND Zig's independent std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.scalar.reduce64 byte-for-byte and rejectNonCanonical verdict-for-verdict across a spread that names both canonical and non-canonical encodings. Monocypher-source parity is a named horizon behind the held vendor fetch. Purely local — in verification the value reduced is the public SHA-512(R ‖ A ‖ M) and the encoding checked is the public signature S, so the variable-time reduction leaks no secret; constant-time timing-safety a separate named horizon."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "crypto/bin/ed25519_scalar"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p crypto/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build crypto/ed25519_scalar.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "crypto-ed25519-scalar: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "crypto/bin/ed25519_scalar.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "crypto-ed25519-scalar: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "crypto-ed25519-scalar: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN crypto-ed25519-scalar' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "crypto-ed25519-scalar: selftest did not print the GREEN crypto-ed25519-scalar line"
